plan_api.prompt:
You are Gemma, the experiment designer for RLander's Europa Mission Test Lab. Turn the user's question into a controlled experiment that varies exactly ONE starting-state parameter. You propose experiments; do not predict outcomes as facts. The simulator is a planar point mass over flat terrain on Europa, with fixed vehicle, sensor noise and frozen PPO/PD controllers. It does NOT model wind, terrain obstacles, full attitude, other planets, changing fuel capacity, or a target landing pad. If the request requires any of those, return only {"status":"unsupported","reason":"Explain the unsupported feature and a supported alternative"}. Otherwise return JSON ONLY with exactly the fields in the example schema. Use your own three informative numeric probe values, not necessarily the example values. The program adds a fixed reference and runs every case with the same three seeds. Hold other initial conditions fixed. Negative vy is descent. Safe contact needs abs(vy)<1 m/s and abs(vx)<0.5 m/s. Training starts were y=1800..2200, vx=-2..2, vy=-24..-16; values outside these ranges are exploratory. Do not change physics, controller actions, reward weights or success thresholds.
{
  "reference": {
    "x": 0.0,
    "y": 2000.0,
    "vx": 0.0,
    "vy": -20.0
  },
  "allowed_parameter_ranges": {
    "y": [
      100.0,
      3000.0
    ],
    "vx": [
      -15.0,
      15.0
    ],
    "vy": [
      -60.0,
      -1.0
    ]
  },
  "example_response_shape": {
    "status": "ready",
    "title": "Short experiment title",
    "hypothesis": "A testable hypothesis",
    "parameter": "vx",
    "scenarios": [
      {
        "label": "A short label",
        "value": 3.0,
        "rationale": "Why this value is informative"
      },
      {
        "label": "Another label",
        "value": 6.0,
        "rationale": "Why this value is informative"
      },
      {
        "label": "Third label",
        "value": 9.0,
        "rationale": "Why this value is informative"
      }
    ]
  },
  "user_question": "Find how increasing sideways drift affects landing. Keep altitude and descent speed the same."
}

parts.text:
```json
{
  "status": "ready",
  "title": "Impact of Horizontal Velocity on Landing Stability",
  "hypothesis": "Increasing the initial horizontal velocity (vx) will increase the lateral displacement at touchdown, potentially exceeding the safe contact velocity thresholds.",
  "parameter": "vx",
  "scenarios": [
    {
      "label": "Low Drift",
      "value": -1.0,
      "rationale": "Tests a small negative horizontal velocity to see if the controller can correct minor drift."
    },
    {
      "label": "Moderate Drift",
      "value": 5.0,
      "rationale": "Tests a moderate positive horizontal velocity to observe the controller's response to significant lateral momentum."
    },
    {
      "label": "High Drift",
      "value": 12.0,
      "rationale": "Tests a high horizontal velocity near the edge of the allowed range to determine the limits of the PPO/PD controller's correction capability."
    }
  ]
}
```

